      "writtenOn": "2024-01-29T19:49:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e \u003e I don\u0027t think this actually changes behavior?\n\u003e \n\u003e It depends upon the caller. The interpolate() case is the one place assuming the current behavior and the one place I changed to account for this. At least in SkPath, interpolate() is the only place that calls addpath(), so fixing it should result in no outward change in behavior.\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003e It\u0027s the same as just doing an assignment, right? The path will later detect the non-unique ownership of the path-ref, and make a copy at the time of edit:\n\u003e \u003e https://fiddle.skia.org/c/b48d312fab531f6e1f8e665f16dffe51\n\u003e \n\u003e That\u0027s right.\n\nJust to clarify on the change. The behavior change is only visible internally, externally this should result in the same SkPath. Any internal code expecting SkPath::addpath() to create a new SkPathRef has to be updated, which is why I changed interpolate(). AFIACT that\u0027s the only place that was expecting a new SkPathRef to be created.",
